This article from the International Cheese Federation discusses a prolonged period of stagnant national milk production growth, which has remained under 1% year-over-year for five months. It explains that this ''barely growing'' state, while not a crisis, signifies a lack of herd rebuilding within the dairy industry, making the supply chain vulnerable to disruptions. The piece advises cheese producers to re-evaluate milk contracts and cost assumptions, and encourages retailers to proactively discuss potential wholesale price shifts with suppliers. It highlights that smaller operations are particularly susceptible to these subtle market tightenings and emphasizes the federation''s role in facilitating the exchange of ground-level intelligence among members.
 
